// bisect: divergence (interp != jit) — FIXED, kept as regress pin.
// The INTERP was the wrong one here too.
// Found by soak aug14c (hz0 fuzz divergence_000002, katana fuzz
// divergence_000000 — two boxes, same shape), 2026-08-14, campaign
// binary 0b7fd7ef; survives the sleep-ride fix (0ba39657). Fuzzer-
// minimized.
//
//   interp: [1:i64:0  2:<array::map fn value>  3:i64:0  4:<fn value>]
//   jit:    [1:i64:0  2:<array::map fn value>  3:i64:4  4:<fn value>]
//
// m = x % 2 alternates 1,0,1,0, so the select alternates between the
// `_` arm (the i64 block) and the arm holding a FUNCTION VALUE.
// Event 1 takes the `_` arm: s is 0 and the connect schedules s <- 4
// for the next cycle — but on that next cycle the arm is ASLEEP (the
// map arm is selected). Event 3 re-selects the `_` arm: the JIT reads
// s = 4 (the scheduled write landed while the arm slept), the interp
// reads s = 0 (as if the write never happened).
//
// The rule to derive: does a scheduled `<-` write land while the target
// binding's arm is asleep? Sleep is PAUSE (Eric 2026-07-31) and a
// connect write is a runtime event on the BINDING, not a node
// computation, which argues the JIT is right — but say so explicitly
// before touching either engine.
//
// Ingredients (both required):
//   - an arm holding a FUNCTION VALUE (array::map): replacing it with
//     i64:1 agrees.
//   - an arm block that binds a local and connects to it: a plain
//     {let s = i64:5; s + i64:1} arm agrees.
//
// ROOT CAUSE (confirmed 2026-08-14 by shell bisect): the arm-wake
// forced init view (node/select.rs:388-391) RE-EXECUTES the arm's seed
// bind. `let s = i64:100` fires under the forced init and republishes
// the seed, clobbering the connect-written value; the kernel treats the
// connect target as a LIFTED state word whose seed applies only at a
// genuine init, so it keeps the accumulated value.
//
// Receipts (same program, distinctive seed 100, connect value 6):
//   alternating (arm sleeps + wakes): interp 100 <fn> 100 <fn>
//                                     jit    100 <fn>   6 <fn>
//   arm ALWAYS selected (no sleep):   BOTH   100 6 6 6
//   self-referential `s <- s + i64:1`: BOTH reseed — so it is not the
//                                     connect, it is the wake.
//
// ADJUDICATION: the KERNEL is right. Sleep is PAUSE (Eric 2026-07-31):
// a paused arm keeps its value-channel state, and a select arm's `let`
// is a NODE in the graph, not a stack slot re-entered per selection —
// the always-selected control shows both engines agree the seed applies
// once. The interp's reseed-on-wake is the bug.
//
// FIX: the wake still forces a REAL init view (kernels force their
// input view off it, call sites prime, refs read standing entries as
// Fired) but now flags it as a WAKE — `Event::wake_init` — and a bind
// that is a `<-` target and already holds a value skips its own
// initializer under that flag. Making the wake a read-only view instead
// was tried first and broke the KERNEL side of this very witness: a
// fused arm kernel needs the forced init to marshal its inputs. Same seam as [rec_arm_let_missing_fire.gx] from the opposite
// side: there the re-execution CANNOT happen (frame) and a local never
// materializes; here it happens when it should not and clobbers state.
//
{let x = array::iter([i64:1, i64:2, i64:3, i64:4]); let m = x % i64:2; let sel = select m {i64:0 => array::map, _ => {let s = i64:0; s <- array::fold([i64:1, i64:2, i64:1], i64:0, |acc, e| acc + e); s}}; sel}
